WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.787
Friday. Five o'clock.

00:00:02.807 --> 00:00:03.877
You ship.

00:00:04.427 --> 00:00:06.113
There's no QA team.

00:00:06.213 --> 00:00:08.719
The people who built it tested it.

00:00:08.819 --> 00:00:11.517
Logged in. On their own laptops.

00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:15.373
So the first stranger to try your sign-up
is a customer.

00:00:15.673 --> 00:00:18.423
And when it fails, they don't file a bug.

00:00:19.373 --> 00:00:20.451
They leave.

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:23.882
Unless someone tests it first.

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:25.241
That's what we do.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:29.921
Together, we pick about 10 flows your
customers rely on.

00:00:30.021 --> 00:00:32.781
Written in plain language you confirm.

00:00:33.031 --> 00:00:37.752
Before every release, our AI agent walks
them like a real user.

00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:40.791
From your CI, or with one click.

00:00:40.891 --> 00:00:44.938
We write the tests, and we keep them
working as your product changes.

00:00:45.288 --> 00:00:49.452
When a test goes red, a person on our team
looks at it first.

00:00:49.552 --> 00:00:51.959
Is it your app, or our test?

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:54.283
We answer that before you see it.

00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:56.245
What you get is simple.

00:00:56.665 --> 00:01:00.005
A green run. Or a short list of real
defects,

00:01:00.025 --> 00:01:02.000
with steps and screenshots.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.663
We run dedicated research batches to track
the state of the market.

00:01:06.763 --> 00:01:11.278
In our latest, we signed up for 28 SaaS
products as strangers.

00:01:11.728 --> 00:01:14.401
24 had a defect in sign-up.

00:01:14.751 --> 00:01:18.659
From you: a test account, and about an
hour a week.

00:01:18.759 --> 00:01:21.397
Start with a 6-week managed pilot.

00:01:21.497 --> 00:01:22.823
Then you decide.

00:01:23.173 --> 00:01:24.914
Book a 30-minute call.

00:01:25.014 --> 00:01:28.267
We look at your product first.
